From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: sun8i: filter the trend the A733 hands to step_wise

The driver supplies no .get_trend, so the core derives one from two
consecutive samples (thermal_helpers.c, get_tz_trend()). On the A733 that
input cannot carry the decision, and the governor built on it has never
converged on this part.

WHAT THE SENSOR DELIVERS AS THIS DRIVER PROGRAMS IT

Cadence. The period is our programming, not a property of the sensor.
sun50i_h6_thermal_init(), which the A733 entry reuses unchanged, writes
THS_PER (the driver's SUN50I_H6_THS_PC, offset 0x08) = 365 at 24 MHz -- the
H6 value, carried over, never chosen for this part -- so a conversion takes
4096 * 366 / 24e6 = 62.464 ms (A733 UM V0.92, THS_PER THERMAL_PER[31:12];
the 24 MHz is set by clk_set_rate() in this driver). THS_FILTER is written FILTER_EN | FILTER_TYPE(1)
= average of four, and the part has five sensors
(A733 user manual), so a new word lands in each THSn_DATA every
4096 * 366 * 4 * 5 / 24e6 = 1.2493 s.

Neither reference programs it that slowly. The UM's own init sequence
writes 0x3A = 58 (UM V1.00 7.1.6.1 Step 5, p. 344 / PDF p. 365; also the
register's reset default, "10ms", p. 346 / PDF p. 367), which by the same
arithmetic is 4096 * 59 * 4 * 5 / 24e6 = 0.201 s per sensor. The vendor
driver writes 28 (sunxi_thermal.c, sun60iw2_thermal_init()), 0.099 s.
Whether a shorter period changes the noise figures below has not been
measured; this patch does not touch THS_PER.

Measured on the board, two independent ways, 2026-08-15: every reported
value holds for 1.22..1.26 s on all five sensors across three runs, and the
shared data interrupt counts 4.00 /s -- five sensors at 0.8 Hz. The zone
polls at 500 ms, so at THS_PER = 365 two out of three governor cycles carry
no new sensor data at all. Shortening polling-delay-passive therefore cannot
help at this period; below 1.25 s the extra cycles are empty, not merely
noisier.

Noise. Detrended inside sliding 10 s windows -- one board thermal time
constant -- and pooled over six full-load runs, the residual is 0.93 K RMS
on ths 0 and 1.23 K RMS on ths 3, with 90th-percentile peak-to-peak of 4.9 K
and 6.3 K. (Quoted by SENSOR INDEX on purpose. These runs predate the
sensor-to-cluster correction in `arm64-dts-a733-thermal-zones-and-cpu-throttling`, so the logs label ths 0 "cpub" and ths 3
"cpul"; after `arm64-dts-a733-thermal-zones-and-cpu-throttling` the same two sensors carry the opposite names. The index is
the invariant, so the numbers are anchored to it.) The same magnitude appears on the DDR, NPU
and GPU sensors under a CPU-only constant load, which those dies cannot be
following, and the residuals are essentially uncorrelated between sensors
(pairwise r 0.02..0.29), so it is not a shared reference either. It is about
twenty times the 59 mC quantum of the conversion, so quantisation is not the
thing to filter. The physical origin is not established; the control
conclusion does not depend on it.

WHAT step_wise DOES WITH THAT

It steps the cooling device up on a noise peak, and on the first sample that
falls below the trip's hysteresis it returns instance->lower -- the whole way
back, in one poll, not one step down. Measured consequence: every throttling
episode ever recorded on this part ended that way, 8 of 8 in a 300 s run and
78 of 78 in a 15 min run, and under degraded airflow cooling states of 4 and
6 were discarded in a single poll with the die at 92 and 93 C. The
controller has never once settled on a state; it oscillates between full
clock and one OPP down, and the die peaks 2.0 K above the 90 C trip.

THE FILTER

Track the highest reading seen. Report RAISING on every new maximum,
DROPPING once the reading has fallen trend_hyst below that maximum, STABLE
in between.

The shape is chosen for two properties an average or a slope cannot give:

  - The first sample at any temperature is always a new maximum, so RAISING
    is reported there. Mitigation at a temperature the loop has not answered
    yet is never delayed -- by construction, at any ramp rate. The measured
    worst-case ramp on this board is 4715 mC/s; a low-pass with a 4 s time
    constant would be blind to 19 K of it.
  - The samples that report DROPPING are a subset of those the core's own
    comparison reports. (If T <= ref - hyst then T < ref; and if the previous
    sample did not report DROPPING then T_prev > ref - hyst >= T, so
    T < T_prev and the unfiltered code reports DROPPING too.) Cooling is
    never given back in a cycle where the unfiltered code would have kept it.

The 110 C critical trip is untouched either way: step_wise skips
THERMAL_TRIP_CRITICAL, and the core acts on it directly. No .get_trend can
delay a shutdown.

trend_hyst = 6000 mC IS DERIVED, NOT PICKED

The rule: the hysteresis has to exceed the excursion the sensor's own noise
produces within one plant time constant, or noise alone fakes a DROPPING.
Measured, pooled over six full-load runs, 10 s windows, detrended:

    sensor      RMS      p2p median   p2p p90    p2p p95
    ths 0       0.93 K      3141 mC    4926 mC    5655 mC
    ths 3       1.23 K      4226 mC    6268 mC    6988 mC

6000 mC sits at the ths 3 p90 and above the ths 0 p95. ⚠ Both figures are
per SENSOR, and by index, for the reason given above: the zone NAMES attached
to these two sensors are swapped by `arm64-dts-a733-thermal-zones-and-cpu-throttling`. The constant itself is unaffected --
it is one value chosen to cover the worse of the two, and the pair of sensors
is the same pair whichever names they wear. Do not re-derive it from the
renaming. Replayed
against the recorded traces it takes the rate of DROPPING reported while the
local regression slope is non-negative -- noise-only releases -- from 68.3
and 64.1 per 300 s to 1.1 and 0.1: factors of 62 and 640. 7000 mC (the p95)
buys 1.1 -> 0.5 and pushes the release level to 7 K below the peak, which
starts to read as never releasing. The knee is at 6000.

One constant for the chip rather than one per zone, and sized on the worse
zone as that requires. The two differ by 1.27x; three of the five zones
carry only a critical trip and never reach this callback at all; and
over-damping the quieter zone costs at most one ladder step on a zone that
throttles for about 1 s in 300. Five numbers where one suffices, four of
them unprovable, is not a refinement.

Two sanity bounds the number also has to clear, and does: it is three times
the 2000 mC hysteresis the device tree gives the trip, so the filter can only
tighten that hysteresis and never widen it; and it is 60 % of the 10 K gap
between the cpufreq trip at 90 C and the idle-injection trip at 100 C, so the
two passive stages stay distinguishable.

THE PRICE, STATED

A larger hysteresis holds the cooling state longer, and that costs clock.

  - Steady state: bounded by one ladder step held where today's controller
    would have thrown it away -- 1794 -> 1716 MHz on CPU-little (-4.3 %),
    2002 -> 1950 MHz on CPU-big (-2.6 %).
  - Release latency on a genuine cool-down: trend_hyst / |dT/dt|, so 12 s at
    the measured end-of-load rate in the worst case. In all six recorded
    cool-downs it was one poll, because the first update after the load ends
    is already more than 6 K down.
  - An open-loop replay of the recorded traces puts the mean frequency
    100..150 MHz lower and, notably, that figure does not change between
    3000 and 8000 mC: the cost is incurred by having a filter at all, not by
    its size. That replay cannot be trusted for an equilibrium -- it feeds a
    fixed temperature to a controller whose whole job is to change it -- so
    the number to watch on hardware is the run-average frequency, not this.

Some of that cost is not a loss. Today's run-average includes time at full
clock that the controller had decided to throttle and then discarded on a
noise sample.

MECHANICS

get_tz_trend() is called once per non-critical trip per governor cycle, and
the A733 CPU zones carry two of them. A callback that advanced its state per
call would hand the second trip a STABLE the first one consumed, which would
silently disable the 100 C idle-injection stage while passing every build and
every review. The state machine is therefore keyed on a sequence number
bumped by ->get_temp, so it advances at most once per sample.

->get_temp only publishes the sample; it does not advance the filter. sysfs
and hwmon reads reach ->get_temp too, and a reader must not be able to change
what the governor sees -- least of all the 4 Hz debug instrumentation used to measure this
patch. A sysfs read cannot steal a transition either: the whole update cycle
and every sysfs read take the same zone mutex, so no read can interleave
between the two ->get_trend calls of one cycle.

trend_hyst = 0 -- every chip in this driver except the A733 -- returns
-EOPNOTSUPP, and get_tz_trend() falls back to the comparison it has always
used. The other nine parts are unaffected by construction, not by review.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED

Applies clean at fuzz 0 on pristine 6.18.36 with `mfd-axp20x-Constify-axp313-and-axp717-cells` to `arm64-dts-a733-bank-pc-io-supply`.
Builds: drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.o, native aarch64, W=1, no warnings;
checkpatch 0 errors. Artifact checked rather than the exit code -- the object
carries sun8i_ths_get_trend.

NOT verified on hardware. The pending observable, in one line: the number of
one-poll transitions of cpufreq-cpu0/cur_state from non-zero to 0 while the
zone was still at or above 88000 mC on the previous sample must fall by at
least half, with no rise in peak die temperature and no fall in the
run-average CPU frequency beyond the one-OPP bound above. The A/B pair is one
test run with two kernel variants: with and without this patch.

The 6000 mC itself rests on an open-loop replay of traces produced by a
different controller. It bounds the direction of the change, not its
equilibrium. If the test boot shows residual chatter, the next knob is the
hardware filter depth (FILTER_TYPE 01 -> 10, four to eight samples), not a
larger trend_hyst.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
diff -uNr a/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c b/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
--- a/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
@@ -63,6 +63,16 @@
 	struct ths_device		*tmdev;
 	struct thermal_zone_device	*tzd;
 	int				id;
+	/*
+	 * Trend filter state, see sun8i_ths_get_trend().  Touched only from
+	 * ->get_temp and ->get_trend, both of which the thermal core calls
+	 * with the zone mutex held, so no further serialisation is needed.
+	 */
+	int				last_temp;
+	int				trend_ref;
+	unsigned int			temp_seq;
+	unsigned int			trend_seq;
+	enum thermal_trend		trend;
 };
 
 struct ths_thermal_chip {
@@ -74,6 +84,14 @@
 	int		scale;
 	int		ft_deviation;
 	int		temp_data_base;
+	/*
+	 * Millidegrees the reading has to fall below the highest value seen
+	 * before ->get_trend reports DROPPING.  Zero -- the default, and what
+	 * every chip but the A733 uses -- disables the callback, so the core
+	 * keeps deriving the trend from two consecutive samples exactly as it
+	 * did before this existed.
+	 */
+	int		trend_hyst;
 	int		(*calibrate)(struct ths_device *tmdev,
 				     u16 *caldata, int callen);
 	int		(*init)(struct ths_device *tmdev);
@@ -170,11 +188,106 @@
 	 */
 	*temp += tmdev->chip->ft_deviation;
 
+	/*
+	 * Publish the sample for ->get_trend.  This deliberately does not
+	 * advance the filter: sysfs and hwmon reads land here too, and a
+	 * reader must not be able to change what the governor sees.
+	 */
+	s->last_temp = *temp;
+	s->temp_seq++;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Trend filtering.
+ *
+ * Without a ->get_trend the core derives the trend from two consecutive
+ * samples.  On this part that is a one-bit differentiator fed a signal it
+ * cannot resolve, for two measured reasons.
+ *
+ * The sensors are slow as this driver programs them -- a choice, not a
+ * property of the part.  sun50i_h6_thermal_init() writes THS_PER = 365 (the
+ * H6 value; the UM's init sequence writes 0x3A = 58, the vendor driver 28)
+ * at 24 MHz, so one conversion takes 4096 * 366 / 24e6 = 62.5 ms; THS_FILTER
+ * averages four of them and the five sensors share the block, which puts a
+ * new word in each THSn_DATA every 4096 * 366 * 4 * 5 / 24e6 = 1.2493 s.
+ * Measured on a Cubie A7S: every reported value holds for 1.22..1.26 s, and
+ * the shared data interrupt fires at 4.00 /s, i.e. 0.8 Hz per sensor.  A CPU
+ * zone polls at 500 ms, so at this period two out of three governor cycles see
+ * no new sensor data at all -- shortening the poll cannot help, and
+ * lengthening it costs response.
+ *
+ * The sensors are noisy.  Detrended inside sliding 10 s windows, pooled over
+ * six full-load runs, the residual on the two CPU zones is 0.93 K and
+ * 1.23 K RMS, with a 90th-percentile peak-to-peak of 4.9 K and 6.3 K.  The
+ * same magnitude appears on the DDR, NPU and GPU sensors under a CPU-only load,
+ * and the residuals are uncorrelated between sensors, so this is not the die
+ * following the workload.  It is roughly twenty times the 59 mC quantum of
+ * the conversion above, which is why quantisation is not the thing to filter.
+ *
+ * step_wise turns that into a controller that never converges: it steps the
+ * cooling device up on a noise peak, and on the first sample that falls below
+ * the trip's hysteresis it takes the device all the way back to
+ * instance->lower in one poll rather than one step down.  Measured on the
+ * same part: every throttling episode ever recorded ended that way -- 8 of 8
+ * in one 300 s run, 78 of 78 in a 15 min run -- and under degraded airflow
+ * cooling states of 4 and 6 were discarded in a single poll with the die at
+ * 92 and 93 degrees.
+ *
+ * So track the highest reading seen and report RAISING on every new maximum,
+ * DROPPING only once the reading has fallen trend_hyst below it, STABLE
+ * otherwise.  Two properties follow, and both are the reason for this shape
+ * rather than an average or a slope:
+ *
+ *  - the first sample at any given temperature is always a new maximum, so
+ *    RAISING is reported there and mitigation at a temperature the loop has
+ *    not answered yet is never delayed.  A filter with a time constant
+ *    cannot say that;
+ *  - the samples that report DROPPING are a subset of those the core's own
+ *    comparison would report, so cooling is never given back in a cycle
+ *    where the unfiltered code would have kept it.
+ *
+ * get_tz_trend() is called once per non-critical trip per governor cycle and
+ * the CPU zones here carry two of them, so the state machine must not run
+ * twice on one sample; it is keyed on the ->get_temp sequence number instead.
+ * A concurrent sysfs read cannot steal a transition, because the whole update
+ * cycle and every sysfs read take the same zone mutex.
+ */
+static int sun8i_ths_get_trend(struct thermal_zone_device *tz,
+			       const struct thermal_trip *trip,
+			       enum thermal_trend *trend)
+{
+	struct tsensor *s = thermal_zone_device_priv(tz);
+	int hyst = s->tmdev->chip->trend_hyst;
+
+	if (!hyst)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	if (s->trend_seq != s->temp_seq) {
+		int temp = s->last_temp;
+
+		s->trend_seq = s->temp_seq;
+
+		if (temp > s->trend_ref) {
+			s->trend_ref = temp;
+			s->trend = THERMAL_TREND_RAISING;
+		} else if (temp <= s->trend_ref - hyst) {
+			s->trend_ref = temp;
+			s->trend = THERMAL_TREND_DROPPING;
+		} else {
+			s->trend = THERMAL_TREND_STABLE;
+		}
+	}
+
+	*trend = s->trend;
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
 static const struct thermal_zone_device_ops ths_ops = {
 	.get_temp = sun8i_ths_get_temp,
+	.get_trend = sun8i_ths_get_trend,
 };
 
 static const struct regmap_config config = {
@@ -560,6 +673,13 @@
 	for (i = 0; i < tmdev->chip->sensor_num; i++) {
 		tmdev->sensor[i].tmdev = tmdev;
 		tmdev->sensor[i].id = i;
+		/*
+		 * Below any real reading, so the first call reports RAISING.
+		 * That call cannot matter: on the first governor cycle every
+		 * instance is still uninitialised and step_wise ignores the
+		 * trend.
+		 */
+		tmdev->sensor[i].trend_ref = THERMAL_TEMP_INVALID;
 		tmdev->sensor[i].tzd =
 			devm_thermal_of_zone_register(tmdev->dev,
 						      i,
@@ -774,6 +894,16 @@
 	.sensor_num = 5,
 	.has_bus_clk_reset = true,
 	.has_mod_clk = true,
+	/*
+	 * 6000 mC is the 90th percentile of the peak-to-peak sensor residual
+	 * measured inside 10 s windows on the noisier of the two CPU zones
+	 * (6268 mC; the quieter one is 4926 mC), pooled over six full-load
+	 * runs.  Sized on the worse zone because one number has to cover
+	 * both.  It leaves the two passive stages 10 K apart distinguishable
+	 * and is three times the hysteresis the device tree gives the trip,
+	 * so the filter can only tighten that hysteresis, never widen it.
+	 */
+	.trend_hyst = 6000,
 	.temp_data_base = SUN50I_H6_THS_TEMP_DATA,
 	.calibrate = sun50i_h6_ths_calibrate,
 	.init = sun50i_h6_thermal_init,
